"""
extract.py — the translator. Turns a consultation transcript (plus, if
available, prescription/document text) into DDXPlus evidence codes:
{code: True} for findings confirmed present, {code: False} for findings
EXPLICITLY denied, code omitted entirely if not discussed at all.

day-plan.md step 5's one hard rule: never guess False for anything not
discussed. Scoring an undiscussed symptom as absent multiplies
P(not-e | pathology) for every symptom nobody asked about, which
systematically penalises exactly the conditions Can't-Miss exists to
surface — the eval would still look fine while the panel quietly suppresses
the diagnoses it's supposed to catch. This file's whole job is to preserve
that three-way distinction; get it wrong and engine.py's math is still
correct but is being fed a lie.

Uses Groq (OpenAI-compatible chat.completions API) with FORCED tool-calling
for structured output. Needs GROQ_API_KEY in .env and `pip install groq`.

PROVIDER HISTORY (each switch driven by a real, verified problem with the
previous one — see git log for the full trail): Anthropic (planned, never
had a key) -> Gemini (google-genai) -> Groq.
  - gemini-3.7-flash worked but 503'd intermittently on the full 223-code
    catalog forced-tool-call, and this key's free tier caps at a plain
    20 requests/DAY for it — too low to be usable at all.
  - gemini-3.1-flash-lite and gemini-3.5-flash-lite were both tried as
    faster/cheaper Gemini alternatives and REJECTED: verified via real
    side-by-side runs against the same mock transcript that both
    fabricate PRESENT findings for topics never discussed at all
    (3.1-flash-lite: E_167 pregnancy status, E_29 family psychiatric
    history with an invented nonexistent value code on a binary field;
    3.5-flash-lite: E_110 "unable to move/get up for 3+ days", and a
    guessed categorical value orphaned from its own parent code). Same
    evidence-corruption failure this file's rule 1 exists to prevent,
    just inverted (fabricated PRESENT instead of guessed ABSENT) — worse,
    not better, regardless of RPD/reliability.

Switched to Groq 2026-08-20 for better rate limits. The real installed
groq==1.6.0 client was introspected first (client.models.list() for what
this key can actually reach, inspect.signature(chat.completions.create)
for real parameter names), then a real forced-tool-call was made and the
actual response inspected before this file was written:
    client.chat.completions.create(model=..., messages=[
        {"role": "system", "content": SYSTEM_PROMPT},
        {"role": "user", "content": user_content}],
        tools=[{"type": "function", "function": {...}}],
        tool_choice={"type": "function", "function": {"name": "record_evidence"}})
    -> resp.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0].function.name / .arguments
       (.arguments is a JSON STRING here, unlike Gemini's already-parsed
       dict — confirmed by inspecting a real response).

BATCHING, and why it's required, not optional: this key's Groq
organization caps EVERY text model (confirmed identically on
openai/gpt-oss-120b, openai/gpt-oss-20b, and qwen/qwen3.6-27b — an
org-wide limit, not a per-model one) at 8000 tokens/minute on the
on_demand tier. The full 223-code catalog alone runs ~15-16.5k tokens
once the system prompt and schema are added — it cannot fit in a single
request on this tier at all, regardless of which model is chosen.
Measured directly (not estimated): 4 of the 223 codes — E_55, E_57,
E_133, E_152, the body-location multi-choice codes with ~165 values
each — account for over 40% of the catalog's serialized size by
themselves (~4270 chars / ~1070 tokens EACH, vs 65-430 chars for
everything else). _batch_catalog() below does simple greedy bin-packing
by serialized character count (12000-char budget per batch, empirically
verified to land every batch comfortably under the 8000-token cap with
room for the response) rather than a fixed code-count-per-batch split,
specifically so the 4 giant codes don't blow out whichever batch they
land in. Each batch is ONE independent forced-tool-call (system prompt +
transcript + doc text repeated per batch, since Groq's chat.completions
has no cross-call state) and results are merged.

gpt-oss-20b (not the larger 120b — same 8000 TPM ceiling either way, no
reason to pay the larger model's latency) also intermittently returns a
400 "Tool choice is required, but model did not call a tool" even with
tool_choice forced — confirmed flaky by retrying the exact same failing
batch immediately and having it succeed. _with_retry treats this
(groq's error code "tool_use_failed") the same as a transient 429/5xx.

KNOWN GAP, confirmed not a prompt-wording problem: 6 of DDXPlus's 15
categorical/multi-choice codes (E_59, E_56, E_58, E_134, E_132, E_136 —
onset-speed and intensity-style 0-10 scales) ship with an EMPTY
value_meaning in release_evidences.json — no V_ labels at all, just bare
integers. _load_catalog() faithfully passes that through as an empty
"values" mapping, so the model has no listed option to match against and
correctly leaves these codes out per rule 3, even when the transcript
clearly discusses onset speed (e.g. "started suddenly"). Checked directly
against the raw JSON, not assumed — this is a real dataset gap, not
something extract.py's prompt can word its way around; a fix would mean
hand-authoring a value_meaning mapping for these 6 codes, out of scope for
today.

Usage:
    from extract import extract
    evidence = extract(transcript_text, doc_text)
    # -> {"E_66": True, "E_100": True, "E_55=V_101": True, "E_91": False, ...}
"""
import json
import os
import time
from pathlib import Path

from dotenv import load_dotenv
load_dotenv()

import groq

MODEL = os.environ.get("GROQ_MODEL", "openai/gpt-oss-20b")
# ^ Verified 2026-08-20 against this project's real GROQ_API_KEY. Not
# openai/gpt-oss-120b: same 8000 TPM org-wide ceiling either way (see
# module docstring's BATCHING section), so the smaller/faster model wins
# with no downside. Override via GROQ_MODEL in .env.

_BATCH_CHAR_BUDGET = 12000
# ^ Empirically verified 2026-08-20: greedy-packed batches at this budget
# used at most ~4700 real prompt_tokens (measured via resp.usage), safely
# under the 8000 TPM cap even after adding the system prompt, transcript,
# schema, and reserved completion tokens.


def _with_retry(fn, *args, max_retries=8, **kwargs):
    """Retries on 429/5xx (rate limit / server error) and Groq's
    "tool_use_failed" 400 (the model ignoring a forced tool_choice —
    confirmed genuinely flaky by retrying an identical failing batch and
    having it succeed immediately, not a fixed input-shape problem)."""
    delay = 2.0
    for attempt in range(max_retries):
        try:
            result = fn(*args, **kwargs)
            if attempt > 0 and os.environ.get("EXTRACT_DEBUG"):
                print(f"  [_with_retry] succeeded on attempt {attempt + 1}")
            return result
        except groq.APIStatusError as e:
            if os.environ.get("EXTRACT_DEBUG"):
                print(f"  [_with_retry] attempt {attempt + 1} failed: {e.status_code} "
                      f"{e.body.get('error', {}).get('code') if isinstance(e.body, dict) else ''}")
            retriable = e.status_code in (429, 500, 502, 503, 504) or (
                e.status_code == 400
                and isinstance(e.body, dict)
                and e.body.get("error", {}).get("code") == "tool_use_failed"
            )
            if retriable and attempt < max_retries - 1:
                time.sleep(delay)
                delay *= 2
                continue
            raise
    raise RuntimeError("unreachable")  # pragma: no cover


_EVIDENCE_PATH = Path(__file__).parent / "ddxplus" / "release_evidences.json"
_catalog_cache = None


def _load_catalog():
    """Compact, English-only view of release_evidences.json for the
    prompt — drops French fields and unused metadata. ~10k tokens for all
    223 codes as of this dataset, checked directly, not estimated."""
    global _catalog_cache
    if _catalog_cache is not None:
        return _catalog_cache
    raw = json.loads(_EVIDENCE_PATH.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    catalog = []
    for code, ev in raw.items():
        entry = {"code": code, "question": ev["question_en"], "type": ev["data_type"]}
        # B = binary, C = categorical (one value from a small fixed set,
        # e.g. speed of onset), M = multi-choice (e.g. location, travel region)
        if ev["data_type"] in ("C", "M"):
            entry["values"] = {v: m.get("en", v)
                                for v, m in ev.get("value_meaning", {}).items()}
            entry["default_value"] = ev.get("default_value")
        catalog.append(entry)
    _catalog_cache = catalog
    return catalog


def _batch_catalog(catalog, char_budget=_BATCH_CHAR_BUDGET):
    """Greedy bin-packing by serialized JSON size, not code count — see
    module docstring's BATCHING section for why (4 of 223 codes are each
    ~20x the size of a typical one)."""
    batches = []
    current, current_chars = [], 0
    for entry in catalog:
        size = len(json.dumps(entry, ensure_ascii=False))
        if current and current_chars + size > char_budget:
            batches.append(current)
            current, current_chars = [], 0
        current.append(entry)
        current_chars += size
    if current:
        batches.append(current)
    return batches


SYSTEM_PROMPT = """You extract structured clinical evidence codes from a \
doctor-patient consultation transcript and, if provided, prescription or \
document text — strictly for a differential-diagnosis engine that expects \
DDXPlus evidence codes as input.

Rules, in order of importance:

1. THREE-VALUED LOGIC. For each code in the catalog, decide: PRESENT (the \
text affirmatively describes this finding), ABSENT (the text explicitly \
denies or rules this out — "no fever", "never smoked"), or UNDISCUSSED \
(anything not clearly one of those two). Only ever record PRESENT or \
ABSENT. Never guess, infer, or default to ABSENT just because something \
wasn't mentioned — leave it out of both lists entirely. This is the single \
most important rule: a wrongly-guessed ABSENT silently penalises exactly \
the diagnoses that would have caused that symptom.

2. Binary ("B" type) codes: present/absent, no value needed.

3. Categorical/multi-choice ("C"/"M" type) codes: only record as present \
if a specific listed value clearly matches what was said (match against \
the code's "values" mapping). If the topic is discussed but no listed \
value clearly fits, leave it out rather than guessing the closest one. \
NEVER record a code's own default_value as present — the default means \
"no finding", not a finding; recording it would be indistinguishable from \
a real finding and would corrupt the evidence the same way a wrong ABSENT \
guess would.

4. Only extract what the given text actually states. Do not use outside \
medical knowledge to infer findings the text didn't mention, even if they \
seem clinically likely. Do not invent a finding just because it seems \
plausible or common for the presenting complaint — if it isn't in the \
text, it does not go in present OR absent.

5. When genuinely uncertain whether to record something, leave it out. \
Omission is always safe here (it becomes UNKNOWN, correctly excluded from \
the likelihood); a wrong guess is not.

You will only ever see a SUBSET of the full evidence catalog in one call \
(it's split across multiple calls for size reasons) — that changes \
nothing about the rules above; judge only the codes shown to you here."""


_TOOL = {
    "type": "function",
    "function": {
        "name": "record_evidence",
        "description": "Record which DDXPlus evidence codes are present or explicitly absent in the given text.",
        "parameters": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "present": {
                    "type": "array",
                    "description": 'Findings confirmed present. Include "value" (a V_ code) only for type C/M codes.',
                    "items": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "properties": {
                            "code": {"type": "string", "description": "e.g. E_66"},
                            "value": {"type": "string", "description": "V_ code, only for C/M type codes"},
                        },
                        "required": ["code"],
                    },
                },
                "absent": {
                    "type": "array",
                    "description": "Findings EXPLICITLY denied in the text (not just unmentioned). Type B codes only.",
                    "items": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "properties": {"code": {"type": "string"}},
                        "required": ["code"],
                    },
                },
            },
            "required": ["present", "absent"],
        },
    },
}


def _extract_batch(client, catalog_batch, transcript, doc_text):
    user_content = (
        f"EVIDENCE CATALOG (JSON array, {len(catalog_batch)} codes — a subset, see system prompt):\n"
        f"{json.dumps(catalog_batch, ensure_ascii=False)}\n\n"
        f"TRANSCRIPT:\n{transcript}\n\n"
        f"DOCUMENT TEXT (prescription/blister strip OCR — may be empty):\n{doc_text or '(none)'}\n\n"
        "Call record_evidence with every code you can confidently classify "
        "as present or absent per the rules. Leave everything else out."
    )
    resp = _with_retry(
        client.chat.completions.create,
        model=MODEL,
        messages=[
            {"role": "system", "content": SYSTEM_PROMPT},
            {"role": "user", "content": user_content},
        ],
        tools=[_TOOL],
        tool_choice={"type": "function", "function": {"name": "record_evidence"}},
        max_completion_tokens=1024,
    )
    # .arguments is a JSON STRING here (unlike Gemini's already-parsed
    # dict — confirmed via a real call, see the module docstring).
    tool_call = resp.choices[0].message.tool_calls[0]
    return json.loads(tool_call.function.arguments)


def extract(transcript, doc_text=""):
    """transcript: a string, OR a list of utterance dicts (each needs some
    combination of speaker/text keys — see the normalization below) such
    as web/index.html's UTT constants or capture.transcribe()'s
    diarized_transcript.entries.
    doc_text: str — e.g. the "text" field from capture.read_image()'s
    return value. Optional; pass "" if there's no document.

    Returns {code: True} / {"CODE=VALUE": True} for present findings,
    {code: False} for explicitly denied findings — matching the format
    demo_case.py's PRESENTING/WITH_RECORDS/ANSWERED dicts use. Codes not
    discussed are omitted entirely.

    Makes one forced-tool-call PER CATALOG BATCH (see module docstring's
    BATCHING section) — not one call total.
    """
    if isinstance(transcript, (list, tuple)):
        lines = []
        for u in transcript:
            speaker = u.get("speaker") or u.get("w") or "?"
            text = u.get("text") or u.get("transcript") or u.get("e") or u.get("orig") or ""
            lines.append(f"{speaker}: {text}")
        transcript = "\n".join(lines)

    client = groq.Groq(api_key=os.environ["GROQ_API_KEY"])
    batches = _batch_catalog(_load_catalog())

    evidence = {}
    for catalog_batch in batches:
        parsed = _extract_batch(client, catalog_batch, transcript, doc_text)
        for item in parsed.get("present", []):
            code = item["code"]
            value = item.get("value")
            key = f"{code}={value}" if value else code
            evidence[key] = True
        for item in parsed.get("absent", []):
            evidence[item["code"]] = False
    return evidence


if __name__ == "__main__":
    import sys
    if sys.platform == "win32":
        sys.stdout.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8")
        sys.stderr.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8")
    if len(sys.argv) < 2:
        print("usage: python extract.py <transcript-text-file> [doc-text-file]")
        sys.exit(1)
    transcript_text = Path(sys.argv[1]).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    doc_text = Path(sys.argv[2]).read_text(encoding="utf-8") if len(sys.argv) > 2 else ""
    result = extract(transcript_text, doc_text)
    print(json.dumps(result, ensure_ascii=False, indent=2))
